7 0 obj
<</Parent 13 0 R/Next 8 0 R/Prev 6 0 R/Title(Conclusion C2: Integrating a managed identity provider ships the gated per-tenant-auth feature with a substantially larger schedule-safety margin against the billed-release deadline than building the same scope on an OSS library)/Dest 387 0 R>>
endobj
8 0 obj
<</Parent 13 0 R/Next 9 0 R/Prev 7 0 R/Title(Conclusion C3: At this company's current scale, the direct vendor cost of buying core per-tenant authentication is negligible-to-zero, and the main future cost driver (enterprise SSO) is metered separately and stays at zero until an enterprise deal requires it)/Dest 388 0 R>>
endobj
9 0 obj
<</Parent 13 0 R/Next 10 0 R/Prev 8 0 R/Title(Conclusion C4: The migration cost of leaving a managed IdP later is bounded and concentrated in enterprise-specific features that do not exist yet at this company's stage, rather than being an immediate, unbounded risk comparable to shipping a security defect today)/Dest 389 0 R>>
endobj
10 0 obj
<</Parent 13 0 R/Next 11 0 R/Prev 9 0 R/Title(Conclusion C5: Buying core authentication preserves, rather than forecloses, this company's option to pursue enterprise customers later, and frees the team's engineering capacity for product work in the meantime)/Dest 390 0 R>>
endobj
11 0 obj
<</Parent 13 0 R/Next 12 0 R/Prev 10 0 R/Title(Conclusion C6: The deadline's urgency is a reason to prefer whichever path has the smaller, better-bounded delivery-time estimate and the lower correctness risk, not a license to accept either path's time or safety claims at face value)/Dest 391 0 R>>
endobj
12 0 obj
<</Parent 13 0 R/Prev 11 0 R/Title(Conclusion C7: Among build, pure-buy, and buy-with-a-thin-internal-adapter, the weighted trade-off favors buying a managed identity provider behind a thin internal adapter interface, deferring enterprise-SSO purchase until an enterprise deal requires it)/Dest 392 0 R>>
endobj
